<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-trigonometric-ratios-of-negative-angles" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Trigonometric Ratios of Negative Angles</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        2.0 Measurement and Geometry
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        2.5 Trigonometry II
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title>
                    <p>
                        Determine trigonometric ratios of negative angles in different situations
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        11
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    12 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To discover, by plotting <m>\theta</m> and <m>-\theta</m> on the unit circle, that the two points are reflections of one another across the <m>x</m>-axis.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                <p>
                    Circulate and ask learners to describe the geometric relationship between the two points <em>before</em> drawing any algebraic conclusions. The key question is: <q>What do you notice about the coordinates of the two points?</q>
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <activity xml:id="angles-going-clockwise">
        <title>Angles Going Clockwise</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Work through the following questions on a unit circle diagram.
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Mark the angle <m>\theta = 60°</m> on the unit circle, measured anticlockwise from the positive <m>x</m>-axis as usual. Call the point <m>P</m>. Write down the exact coordinates of <m>P</m>, and hence state <m>\cos 60°</m> and <m>\sin 60°</m>.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            Now mark the angle <m>-60°</m>, measured <em>clockwise</em> from the positive <m>x</m>-axis. Call this point <m>Q</m>. Write down the exact coordinates of <m>Q</m>.
                        </p>
                        <note component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                <m>P = \left(\dfrac{1}{2}, \dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)</m>, so <m>\cos 60° = \dfrac{1}{2}</m> and <m>\sin 60° = \dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}</m>. <m>Q = \left(\dfrac{1}{2}, -\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)</m>. Prompt learners who are unsure to think of <m>-60°</m> as <q>rotating <m>60°</m> backwards</q> from the positive <m>x</m>-axis.
                            </p>
                        </note>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            How does <m>Q</m> relate to <m>P</m>? Describe the relationship geometrically.
                        </p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                <m>Q</m> is the reflection of <m>P</m> across the <m>x</m>-axis: the <m>x</m>-coordinates are the same and the <m>y</m>-coordinates are negatives of each other.
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Repeat the process with <m>\theta = 120°</m>: mark the points for <m>120°</m> and <m>-120°</m> and write down their coordinates. Then complete the conjecture:
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            For any angle <m>\theta</m>:
                            <me>\cos(-\theta) = \underline{\hspace{2cm}} \qquad \sin(-\theta) = \underline{\hspace{2cm}}</me>
                        </p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                For <m>120°</m>: point is <m>\left(-\dfrac{1}{2}, \dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)</m>.
                                For <m>-120°</m>: point is <m>\left(-\dfrac{1}{2}, -\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)</m>.
                            </p>
                            <p>
                                Conjecture: <m>\cos(-\theta) = \cos\theta</m> and <m>\sin(-\theta) = -\sin\theta</m>.
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </activity>

    <!-- TODO: Add a JSXGraph interactive showing θ and -θ simultaneously on the unit circle,
         with both points labelled, their coordinates displayed side by side, and a slider
         for θ so learners can verify the reflection symmetry for any angle. -->

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    10 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To formalise the reflection argument into the negative-angle identities.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                <p>
                    Ask: <q>Reflecting across the <m>x</m>-axis leaves the <m>x</m>-coordinate unchanged and negates the <m>y</m>-coordinate. Since the coordinates of a unit circle point are <m>(\cos\theta, \sin\theta)</m>, what must <m>\cos(-\theta)</m> and <m>\sin(-\theta)</m> equal?</q>
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <p>
        So far we have defined angles as positive when measured anticlockwise from the positive <m>x</m>-axis. A <term>negative angle</term> is measured <em>clockwise</em> instead. For example, <m>-60°</m> is a <m>60°</m> rotation clockwise from the positive <m>x</m>-axis, landing in the fourth quadrant.
    </p>
    <p>
        On the unit circle, the point for <m>-\theta</m> is directly below (or above) the point for <m>\theta</m><mdash/>it is the <em>reflection of <m>(\cos\theta, \sin\theta)</m> across the <m>x</m>-axis</em>. Reflection across the <m>x</m>-axis leaves the <m>x</m>-coordinate unchanged and negates the <m>y</m>-coordinate, so:
        <md>
            <mrow>\cos(-\theta) \amp= \cos\theta</mrow>
            <mrow>\sin(-\theta) \amp= -\sin\theta</mrow>
        </md>
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-negative-angle-reflection">
        <caption>Point <m>P = (\cos\theta, \sin\theta)</m> and its reflection <m>Q = (\cos\theta, -\sin\theta)</m> across the <m>x</m>-axis, corresponding to angles <m>\theta</m> and <m>-\theta</m> respectively.</caption>
        <image width="65%">
            <shortdescription>Unit circle with point P at angle theta in Q1 and point Q (reflection of P across the x-axis) at angle negative theta in Q4, connected by a dashed vertical line.</shortdescription>
            <latex-image>
                \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \pgfmathsetmacro{\rr}{2.5}
                    \pgfmathsetmacro{\angval}{55}
                    \pgfmathsetmacro{\pxx}{\rr*cos(\angval)}
                    \pgfmathsetmacro{\pyy}{\rr*sin(\angval)}
                    \pgfmathsetmacro{\halfangval}{\angval/2}
                    \pgfmathsetmacro{\axlen}{1.2*\rr}

                    % Unit circle
                    \draw[thick] (0,0) circle (\rr);

                    % Axes
                    \draw[-&gt;] (-\axlen,0) -- (\axlen,0) node[right] {$x$};
                    \draw[-&gt;] (0,-\axlen) -- (0,\axlen) node[above] {$y$};

                    % Dashed vertical line from P to Q (reflection axis is the x-axis)
                    \draw[dashed,gray!60] (\pxx,\pyy) -- (\pxx,-\pyy);

                    % Radii OP and OQ
                    \draw[blue!70!black, thick] (0,0) -- (\pxx, \pyy);
                    \draw[red!70!black,  thick] (0,0) -- (\pxx,-\pyy);

                    % Small angle arcs near origin
                    \draw[blue!70!black, thick] (0.55,0) arc (0:\angval:0.55);
                    \draw[red!70!black,  thick] (0.55,0) arc (0:{-\angval}:0.55);

                    % Angle labels at midpoint of each arc
                    \node[blue!70!black] at (\halfangval:0.95) {$\theta$};
                    \node[red!70!black]  at (-\halfangval:0.95) {$-\theta$};

                    % Points P and Q
                    \filldraw[blue!70!black] (\pxx, \pyy)  circle (2.5pt)
                        node[above right] {$P$};
                    \filldraw[red!70!black]  (\pxx,-\pyy) circle (2.5pt)
                        node[below right] {$Q$};

                    % Origin
                    \node[below left] at (0,0) {$O$};
                \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
        From these two identities we can immediately derive the rule for tangent:
        <me>\tan(-\theta) = \frac{\sin(-\theta)}{\cos(-\theta)} = \frac{-\sin\theta}{\cos\theta} = -\tan\theta.</me>
    </p>

    <insight>
        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        A <term>negative angle</term> is measured clockwise from the positive <m>x</m>-axis. The angle <m>-\theta</m> has the same terminal side as <m>360° - \theta</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The <term>negative-angle identities</term> are:
                        <md>
                            <mrow>\cos(-\theta) \amp= \cos\theta</mrow>
                            <mrow>\sin(-\theta) \amp= -\sin\theta</mrow>
                            <mrow>\tan(-\theta) \amp= -\tan\theta</mrow>
                        </md>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        In words: negating the angle leaves cosine unchanged, but changes the sign of sine and tangent.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </insight>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example>
        <title>Exact Values for a Negative Angle</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the exact values of <m>\sin(-60°)</m>, <m>\cos(-60°)</m>, and <m>\tan(-60°)</m>.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-negative-sixty-degrees">
                <caption>The angle <m>-60°</m> measured clockwise (red). Point <m>Q</m> is the reflection of <m>P</m> (at <m>60°</m>, dashed blue) across the <m>x</m>-axis.</caption>
                <image width="48%">
                    <shortdescription>Unit circle showing the point at negative 60 degrees in Q4 (red), with a dashed comparison to the 60-degree point in Q1 (blue).</shortdescription>
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}
                            \pgfmathsetmacro{\rr}{2.2}
                            \pgfmathsetmacro{\ang}{60}
                            \pgfmathsetmacro{\pxx}{\rr*cos(\ang)}
                            \pgfmathsetmacro{\pyy}{\rr*sin(\ang)}
                            \pgfmathsetmacro{\axlen}{1.25*\rr}
                            % Unit circle and axes
                            \draw[thick] (0,0) circle (\rr);
                            \draw[-&gt;] (-\axlen,0) -- (\axlen,0) node[right] {$x$};
                            \draw[-&gt;] (0,-\axlen) -- (0,\axlen) node[above] {$y$};
                            % Dashed reflection line
                            \draw[dashed,gray!60] (\pxx,\pyy) -- (\pxx,-\pyy);
                            % Reference radius (60 degrees, blue dashed)
                            \draw[blue!50!black, dashed] (0,0) -- (\pxx,\pyy);
                            \draw[blue!40!black, dashed] (0.5,0) arc (0:\ang:0.5);
                            \node[blue!60!black, font=\small] at ({\ang/2}:0.78) {$60°$};
                            \filldraw[blue!50!black] (\pxx,\pyy) circle (2pt)
                                node[above right, font=\small] {$P$};
                            % Main radius (-60 degrees, red)
                            \draw[red!70!black, thick] (0,0) -- (\pxx,-\pyy);
                            % Clockwise arc for -60 degrees
                            \draw[red!70!black, thick] (0.65,0) arc (0:{-\ang}:0.65);
                            \node[red!70!black, font=\small] at ({-\ang/2}:0.95) {$-60°$};
                            % Point Q
                            \filldraw[red!70!black] (\pxx,-\pyy) circle (2.5pt)
                                node[below right, font=\small] {$Q$};
                            \node[below left, font=\small] at (0,0) {$O$};
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Applying the negative-angle identities:
                <md>
                    <mrow>\sin(-60°) \amp= -\sin 60° = -\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\cos(-60°) \amp= \cos 60° = \frac{1}{2}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\tan(-60°) \amp= -\tan 60° = -\sqrt{3}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <title>Finding Ratios of a Negative Angle from Given Values</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose <m>\sin\theta = \dfrac{5}{13}</m> and <m>\cos\theta = \dfrac{12}{13}</m>. Find <m>\sin(-\theta)</m>, <m>\cos(-\theta)</m>, and <m>\tan(-\theta)</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Applying the negative-angle identities directly:
                <md>
                    <mrow>\sin(-\theta) \amp= -\sin\theta = -\frac{5}{13}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\cos(-\theta) \amp= \cos\theta = \frac{12}{13}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\tan(-\theta) \amp= -\tan\theta = -\frac{\sin\theta}{\cos\theta} = -\frac{5/13}{12/13} = -\frac{5}{12}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <title>Combining Negative-Angle Identities with CAST</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Evaluate <m>\sin(-150°) + \cos(-240°)</m> exactly.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Apply the negative-angle identities first:
                <md>
                    <mrow>\sin(-150°) \amp= -\sin 150°</mrow>
                    <mrow>\cos(-240°) \amp= \cos 240°</mrow>
                </md>
                Now evaluate each using reference angles and the CAST rule.
            </p>
            <p>
                <m>\sin 150°</m>: The reference angle for <m>150°</m> (Q2) is <m>180° - 150° = 30°</m>. Sine is positive in Q2, so <m>\sin 150° = \sin 30° = \dfrac{1}{2}</m>. Hence <m>\sin(-150°) = -\dfrac{1}{2}</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                <m>\cos 240°</m>: The reference angle for <m>240°</m> (Q3) is <m>240° - 180° = 60°</m>. Cosine is negative in Q3, so <m>\cos 240° = -\cos 60° = -\dfrac{1}{2}</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                Therefore:
                <me>\sin(-150°) + \cos(-240°) = -\frac{1}{2} + \left(-\frac{1}{2}\right) = -1.</me>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <title>Simplifying a Trigonometric Expression</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Show that <m>\dfrac{\sin(-\theta)}{\cos(-\theta)} + \tan\theta = 0</m> for any angle <m>\theta</m> (where the expression is defined).
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Apply the negative-angle identities to the left-hand side:
                <md>
                    <mrow>\frac{\sin(-\theta)}{\cos(-\theta)} + \tan\theta \amp= \frac{-\sin\theta}{\cos\theta} + \tan\theta</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp= -\tan\theta + \tan\theta</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp= 0. \qquad \checkmark</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />

    <exercises>
        <exercise xml:id="negative-angle-exact-values">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the exact value of each expression.
                    <ol marker="(a)" cols="2">
                        <li><p><m>\sin(-45°)</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\cos(-90°)</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\tan(-30°)</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\sin(-180°)</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\cos(-270°)</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\tan(-120°)</m></p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p><m>-\dfrac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>0</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>-\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{3}</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>0</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>0</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\sqrt{3}</m></p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="negative-angle-from-given">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose <m>\sin\alpha = \dfrac{3}{5}</m> and <m>\cos\alpha = \dfrac{4}{5}</m>. Find each of the following.
                    <ol marker="(a)" cols="2">
                        <li><p><m>\sin(-\alpha)</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\cos(-\alpha)</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\tan(-\alpha)</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\sin(-\alpha) + \cos(-\alpha)</m></p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p><m>-\dfrac{3}{5}</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\dfrac{4}{5}</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>-\dfrac{3}{4}</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>-\dfrac{3}{5} + \dfrac{4}{5} = \dfrac{1}{5}</m></p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="negative-angle-simplify">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Simplify each expression. Your answer should not involve negative angles.
                    <ol marker="(a)" cols="2">
                        <li><p><m>\sin(-\theta) + \sin\theta</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\cos(-\theta) - \cos\theta</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\tan(-\theta) \cdot \tan\theta</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\sin^2(-\theta) + \cos^2(-\theta)</m></p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p><m>-\sin\theta + \sin\theta = 0</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\cos\theta - \cos\theta = 0</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>(-\tan\theta)(\tan\theta) = -\tan^2\theta</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>(-\sin\theta)^2 + \cos^2\theta = \sin^2\theta + \cos^2\theta = 1</m></p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="negative-angle-multistep">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    An angle <m>\theta</m> is acute and satisfies <m>\sin(-\theta) = -\dfrac{8}{17}</m>.
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>Find <m>\sin\theta</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Use the Pythagorean Identity to find <m>\cos\theta</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Find <m>\tan(-\theta)</m>.</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>Since <m>\sin(-\theta) = -\sin\theta = -\dfrac{8}{17}</m>, we have <m>\sin\theta = \dfrac{8}{17}</m>.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><m>\cos^2\theta = 1 - \dfrac{64}{289} = \dfrac{225}{289}</m>. Since <m>\theta</m> is acute, <m>\cos\theta = \dfrac{15}{17}</m>.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><m>\tan(-\theta) = -\tan\theta = -\dfrac{8/17}{15/17} = -\dfrac{8}{15}</m>.</p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="negative-angle-equivalence">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A negative angle <m>-\theta</m> has the same terminal side as the positive angle <m>360° - \theta</m>.
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>Express <m>-45°</m> as an equivalent positive angle in <m>[0°, 360°]</m>.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Verify that <m>\sin(-45°) = \sin 315°</m> and <m>\cos(-45°) = \cos 315°</m>, using the identities and the CAST rule.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Express <m>-150°</m> as an equivalent positive angle in <m>[0°, 360°]</m>, and find <m>\tan(-150°)</m> by first converting to the positive angle.</p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p><m>360° - 45° = 315°</m>.</p></li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Using identities: <m>\sin(-45°) = -\sin 45° = -\dfrac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m> and <m>\cos(-45°) = \cos 45° = \dfrac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m>.
                            </p>
                            <p>
                                Using CAST: <m>315°</m> is in Q4 (reference angle <m>45°</m>), so <m>\sin 315° = -\dfrac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m> and <m>\cos 315° = \dfrac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m>. The values agree. <m>\checkmark</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>360° - 150° = 210°</m>. The angle <m>210°</m> is in Q3 with reference angle <m>30°</m>. In Q3, tangent is positive, so <m>\tan 210° = \tan 30° = \dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{3}</m>. Hence <m>\tan(-150°) = \dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{3}</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-methods.ptx" />

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/reflection.ptx"/>
</section>
